  1409	/**Function*************************************************************
  1410	
  1411	  Synopsis    [Returns 1 if a fanout/load proxy should reject this cut.]
  1412	
  1413	  Description [Mode 1 is the classic map -f guard. Mode 2 is the stmap1
  1414	  selective guard, which only rejects wider cuts when the node has higher
  1415	  estimated fanout. Mode 3 is the stmap2 guard, which uses the same fanout
  1416	  thresholds only during area recovery and only when the current phase has
  1417	  slack over the mapper required time. Mode 4 is the stmap3 guard, which
  1418	  narrows the intervention to high-fanout nodes with only shallow positive
  1419	  slack. Mode 5 is the stmap4 split-phase guard: stmap1-style guarding in
  1420	  delay matching and stmap2-style slack-aware guarding in area recovery.
  1421	  Mode 6 is the stmap5 shape-gated split-phase guard: the delay pass only
  1422	  uses stmap1-style guarding in very large mapper graphs, while area recovery
  1423	  keeps the stmap2 slack-aware guard. Mode 7 is the stmap6 area-sensitive
  1424	  shape-gated guard: the delay pass keeps the stmap5 graph-size gate, small
  1425	  mapper graphs keep the stmap2 slack-aware recovery guard, and exact-area
  1426	  recovery on larger graphs checks the cut after matching so materially cheaper
  1427	  cuts can override the fanout/load proxy. Mode 8 is the stmap7 criticality-
  1428	  sensitive version of mode 7: exact-area recovery on larger graphs allows the
  1429	  override only with deeper mapper slack and outside the highest fanout bucket.
  1430	  Mode 9 is the stmap8 arrival-bounded version of mode 8: the area-saving
  1431	  override also requires the candidate match to stay within one quarter of an
  1432	  inverter delay of the existing mapper arrival. Mode 10 is the stmap9
  1433	  middle-slack area-relief version of mode 9: it keeps the highest fanout
  1434	  bucket protected, but in the one-to-two inverter slack bucket it allows a
  1435	  wide cut only when it is arrival-neutral and saves at least one inverter
  1436	  area. Mode 11 is the stmap10 depth-qualified version of mode 10: the
  1437	  middle-slack relief is allowed only for shallow and medium-depth nodes.
  1438	  Mode 12 is the stmap11 tight-depth version of mode 11: the middle-slack
  1439	  relief is allowed only for shallower nodes. Mode 13 is the stmap12
  1440	  fanout-bucket split version of mode 12: the tight-depth middle-slack relief
  1441	  is allowed only in the lower moderate fanout bucket. Mode 14 is stmap13,
  1442	  which keeps mode 13 behavior while recording guard-hit counters. Mode 15 is
  1443	  stmap14, which keeps the same bucket and counters but requires a stronger
  1444	  middle-slack area-flow saving. Mode 16 is stmap15, which restores the
  1445	  one-inverter relief threshold only after the current run has observed a high
  1446	  lower-moderate/middle-slack exact-area profile. Mode 17 is stmap16, which
  1447	  keeps mode 16 behavior and prints per-relief diagnostics. Mode 18 is stmap17,
  1448	  which uses the one-inverter post-profile threshold only for candidates with
  1449	  materially better mapper arrival. Mode 19 is stmap18, which keeps mode 18
  1450	  decisions and adds near-miss diagnostics for candidates rejected by the
  1451	  timing-quality/profile fallback. Mode 20 is stmap19, which allows the
  1452	  one-inverter threshold before profile-open only for candidates with the same
  1453	  material mapper-arrival improvement. Mode 21 is stmap20, which keeps the
  1454	  mode 20 pre-profile seed but restricts it to shallow nodes. Mode 22 is
  1455	  stmap21, which keeps the shallow seed and admits deeper pre-profile seeds
  1456	  only when the arrival gain is unusually large. Mode 23 is stmap22, which
  1457	  relaxes the deep pre-profile seed boundary to a moderate arrival gain. Mode
  1458	  24 is stmap23, which keeps the strong deep seeds but allows moderate deep
  1459	  seeds only when the existing mapper slack is near the critical side of the
  1460	  middle-slack window. Mode 25 is stmap24, which tightens the moderate deep
  1461	  slack gate to the lower edge of that critical window. Mode 26 is stmap25,
  1462	  which keeps stmap24 and admits at most one moderate deep seed from the next
  1463	  slack band as an ablation diagnostic. Mode 27 is stmap26, which skips the
  1464	  first such moderate seed and admits only the second qualifying seed. Mode 28
  1465	  is stmap27, which reopens the next slack band except for the high-reference,
  1466	  low-slack, stronger-moderate signature isolated by stmap26. Mode 29 is
  1467	  stmap28, which replaces that hard signature blocker with a high-reference
  1468	  moderate-seed area penalty that scales with reference count, slack risk, and
  1469	  arrival-gain risk. Mode 30 is stmap29, which keeps the same seed band but
  1470	  makes the penalty a continuous load-risk gradient. Mode 31 is stmap30,
  1471	  which keeps that moderate-seed gradient and adds a smaller continuous
  1472	  penalty to high-reference strong deep seeds. Mode 32 is stmap31, which keeps
  1473	  mode 31's moderate penalty but dampens the strong-seed gain slope. Mode 33
  1474	  is stmap32, which adds a cut-leaf load proxy to the strong-seed penalty. Mode
  1475	  34 is stmap33, which normalizes that load proxy by supergate fanout limit.
  1476	  Mode 35 is stmap34, which uses a bounded sublinear load/drive curve. Mode
  1477	  36 is stmap35, which preserves that mapper curve while command-level SCL
  1478	  load/max-cap diagnostics are emitted. Mode 37 is stmap36, which strengthens
  1479	  the bounded curve according to direct SCL over-cap severity measured by a
  1480	  command-layer first pass. Mode 38 is stmap37, which applies that feedback
  1481	  only when the current mapper node appears in the first-pass over-cap
  1482	  hotspot set. Mode 39 is stmap38, which broadens the local association to
  1483	  command-collected fanout users and candidate cut leaves. Mode 40 is stmap39,
  1484	  which replaces the static hotspot neighborhood with command-collected
  1485	  downstream consumer pressure. Mode 41 is stmap40, which preserves those
  1486	  pressure ratios in a dense AIG-ID lookup so medium-pressure reconstruction
  1487	  IDs are not discarded by a capped table. Mode 42 is stmap41, which uses the
  1488	  same dense transfer but weights pressure by first-pass SCL timing criticality
  1489	  before it can increase the strong-seed area margin. Mode 43 is stmap42, which
  1490	  propagates sink criticality from over-cap consumers into their fanin pressure
  1491	  cones with a bounded raw-pressure floor. Mode 44 is stmap43, which keeps the
  1492	  sink-pressure strong-seed protection but gates moderate deep area penalties
  1493	  by the same sink-pressure vector so unpressured paths can recover area. Mode
