  1664	/**Function*************************************************************
  1665	
  1666	  Synopsis    [Returns 1 if an stmap area-sensitive mode should reject a cut.]
  1667	
  1668	  Description [The area-sensitive guards are evaluated after matching the
  1669	  candidate cut because their hypotheses need the candidate's actual mapper
  1670	  area. In stmap6 exact-area recovery, a wide high-fanout cut is rejected only
  1671	  when the existing match has slack and the candidate does not save at least
  1672	  half an inverter area. In stmap7, the area-saving override additionally
  1673	  requires more than two inverter delays of slack and excludes the highest
  1674	  fanout-risk bucket. In stmap8, the override is further rejected when the
  1675	  candidate match worsens mapper arrival by more than one quarter of an
  1676	  inverter delay. In stmap9, middle-slack candidates can recover area only
  1677	  when they are outside the highest fanout bucket, do not worsen mapper
  1678	  arrival, and save at least one inverter area. In stmap10, that middle-slack
  1679	  relief also requires the mapper node to be no deeper than level 96. In
  1680	  stmap11, that depth window is tightened to level 64. In stmap12, the same
  1681	  tight-depth relief is allowed only in the lower moderate fanout bucket. In
  1682	  stmap13, the same decision path also records exact-area guard statistics. In
  1683	  stmap14, the lower-moderate middle-slack relief must save two inverter areas
  1684	  instead of one. In stmap15, one-inverter relief is restored only after the
  1685	  mapper observes a high lower-moderate/middle-slack profile. In stmap16, the
  1686	  same behavior also reports each admitted relief choice. In stmap17, the
  1687	  adaptive one-inverter relief threshold also requires a material mapper-arrival
  1688	  improvement; arrival-neutral choices keep the stricter two-inverter fallback.
  1689	  In stmap18, the same decisions are preserved while near-miss rejected
  1690	  middle-slack candidates are logged. In stmap19, pre-profile one-inverter
  1691	  relief is seeded only by candidates with the same material arrival gain. In
  1692	  stmap20, the pre-profile seed is further limited to shallow nodes. In
  1693	  stmap21, deeper pre-profile seeds are allowed only with a much larger mapper
  1694	  arrival gain. In stmap22, the same deep-seed path is relaxed to a moderate
  1695	  mapper-arrival gain. In stmap23, moderate deep seeds also need near-critical
  1696	  existing mapper slack. In stmap24, the moderate deep slack gate is tightened
  1697	  to the lower edge of the critical slack window. In stmap25, the stmap24 rule
  1698	  is kept, and one moderate deep seed from the next slack band is admitted for
  1699	  ablation. In stmap26, the first such moderate seed is skipped and only the
  1700	  second qualifying seed is admitted. In stmap27, moderate deep candidates in
  1701	  the next slack band are admitted unless they match the high-reference,
  1702	  low-slack, stronger-moderate signature isolated by stmap26. In stmap28, that
  1703	  hard blocker is replaced by a soft area-margin penalty on high-reference
  1704	  moderate seeds whose slack and arrival-gain ratios resemble load-risky
  1705	  choices. In stmap29, the high-reference penalty is made continuous over the
  1706	  same seed band. In stmap30, the same load-risk surface is extended to
  1707	  high-reference strong deep seeds with a smaller continuous penalty. In
  1708	  stmap31, the strong-seed gain slope is damped to probe the penalty threshold.
  1709	  In stmap32, a cut-leaf load proxy adds a candidate-cut fanout-shape term to
  1710	  the strong-seed penalty. In stmap33, the cut-leaf term is normalized by the
  1711	  selected supergate fanout limit to test a library-derived drive/load signal.
  1712	  In stmap34, the drive-normalized term uses a bounded sublinear curve so high
  1713	  load/drive ratios do not automatically saturate the area margin. Stmap35
  1714	  preserves the stmap34 mapper decision surface and adds direct SCL
  1715	  mapped-load diagnostics in the command layer. Stmap36 feeds the aggregate
  1716	  direct SCL load/max-cap severity from a first pass back into the same bounded
  1717	  strong-seed area margin. Stmap37 restricts that SCL feedback to first-pass
  1718	  over-cap hotspot AIG IDs and their immediate mapped fanin cone. Stmap38
  1719	  broadens the first-pass association and checks candidate cut leaves against
  1720	  that hotspot set before applying the local SCL feedback term. Stmap39 carries
  1721	  first-pass SCL load pressure onto downstream consumers and their fanin cones,
  1722	  then lets strong-seed candidates use the maximum node/cut consumer pressure
  1723	  as the local SCL feedback term. Stmap40 keeps the same pressure source but
  1724	  passes it as a dense AIG-ID vector so medium-pressure reconstructed IDs survive
  1725	  the command-to-mapper boundary. Stmap41 keeps dense pressure but gates its
  1726	  strength by first-pass SCL criticality, reducing non-critical over-cap hits
  1727	  before they affect mapper area recovery. Stmap42 instead propagates the
  1728	  over-cap consumer's criticality into the fanin cone and keeps a bounded
  1729	  raw-pressure floor for sink-correlated load pressure. Stmap43 keeps the
  1730	  strong-seed sink-pressure rule but applies moderate deep penalties only when
  1731	  the candidate or its leaves have material sink-pressure, letting unpressured
  1732	  area-recovery candidates use the lighter one-inverter margin. Stmap44 keeps
  1733	  the same strong-seed pressure protection and tightens moderate recovery by
  1734	  requiring direct node pressure and cut-leaf pressure to agree.]
  1735	
  1736	  SideEffects []
  1737	
  1738	  SeeAlso     []
  1739	
  1740	***********************************************************************/
